A||||18||pHoOl||Water seperator in intake system||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||07-29-2001||02:57 AM||||I have heard of people taking this out on theirs to get better air flow.  Now i'll admit that sometimes i'm off in my thinking, but if you take the water seperator out, wouldn't you have a problem of water getting in the engine?  If not, why the hell did GM waste money putting it in there.<P>How does putting a piece of 3 inch tube into the bottom of the air filter fix this whole thing?<P><P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rogue_Ant||07-29-2001||03:04 AM||||Well they put it in the same reason they give the car understeer -  because sh!t happens.  Now lets be realistic, are you going to directly spray water into your intake, hopefully not.  So for the majority of us smoothing out the intake airflow is worth the extremely mild chance of water intake.<P>Rogue<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Rogue_Ant (edited 07-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||banditbalz||07-29-2001||10:00 AM||||How could a small amount of water in your intake be harmful? I believe it would be benificial. I mist my engine with a fine spray of water every spring to help keep the carbon out of the engine. It's an old hotrodder's secret and iv'e been doing it for years. I took my GT in for emission testing last month and it ran so clean the mechanic asked me if the engine was new. <BR> _<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine_3.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||12
Z||000003||pHoOl||07-29-2001||10:29 AM||||Hmm... i guess that i thought if you got water in there it would seize up.  I've never heard that mist of water trick.  but you say it works, eh?  interesting<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||banditbalz||07-29-2001||10:34 AM||||Engine has to be running and warm. Take an old, clean Windex bottle filled with clean lukewarm water. Set it to fine sray. Remove intake tube aft of the air cleaner and while opening the throttle blades give it a squirt. Keep the rpm's high as to not bog the engine. Also a good time to clean any of the gunk out of the throttle body.<BR>_<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sunset.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!<p>[This message has been edited by banditbalz (edited 07-29-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000005||DecadenceR||07-29-2001||12:17 PM||||I took out my water seperator recently and I'm sure it would prove VERY difficult to actually get any water into the engine, because there's no tube or anything that would allow water to get from the intake vent to the tube. It would just frop out onto the ground because it's all open space once that boz is removed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||07-29-2001||01:52 PM||||Then where is your sucking air from?  If you disconnected the box, do you just have a hose flopping around?  Otherwise, how would there not be something connected?  Sorry if i sound ignorant, but i haven't ripped apart my intake system to take a look at what all goes into it.  <P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||07-29-2001||02:09 PM||||The most logical excuse that some of us have come up with about this "water trap" is that is not more than a muffler for the intake. If it were a true water trap it would be considered upside down. Water would collect at the BOTTOM leaving the air to continue on. So as a water trap, it is pretty ineffectual.<BR>   Replacing the trap with a tube directly connecting the intake grate does allow intake flow to increase. Whether or not this is benificial for more than 5 hp is still in argument. Most of us do, however, take it off simply because of the sound. I have a Holley style intake on mine and though we don't have anything that could resemble seasons in Southern California we do get the occasional rainstorm to break the monotonous 78 degree days in December. In the 6 years of having this set up on my 87 coupe and now my 85 GT I haven't had any problems.<BR>    Oh and for your question about just taking out the box, there is simply space between the intake grate and the hose connector going to the bottom of the filter canister. Nothing is flopping around. The space consists of the body panel in the front, the wheel well and chassis on the sides. Its a pretty isolated space as far as air flow. <P>Hope that clears it up some.||||reg||6
Z||000008||theogre||07-29-2001||02:58 PM||||Unless you stuff a high pressure hose in it, you aren't going to get water up enough to be an issue.<P>The thing is more of a noise muffler than anything.<P>I seem to recall someone here did a dyno run on it and found no diferance in stock vs altered in that location. If there was a dif it was really small.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Pontiaddict||07-29-2001||06:34 PM||||Well, water only has to get to the air filter to cause problems. There's nothing like sucking wet paper into your engine.<BR>If you have a K&N then don't worry about it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||DecadenceR||07-29-2001||06:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pHoOl:<BR><B>Then where is your sucking air from?  If you disconnected the box, do you just have a hose flopping around?  Otherwise, how would there not be something connected?  Sorry if i sound ignorant, but i haven't ripped apart my intake system to take a look at what all goes into it.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The water collector is only a box behind the inner fender that connects to the inside of where the inlet hose attaches. But the hose still attaches to the same place but instead of pulling the air through the box, you get free air from the outside. From inside the engine bay, you can't tell it was taken out my looking.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Capt Fiero||07-29-2001||07:10 PM||||Hey all long time no see, I have a modifed motor (bigger cam, mild porting and better exhaust + K&N filters and what not) I have the Holly scoop on my car also, and when I removed the water box, in place of a piece of heavy tubing, the only improvment that it made in my eyes was really the sound it made. I like the woosh sound as the throttle opens up now.  If you have a bone stock motor you probably wont see any performance diff. But it is an easy and inexpensive mode and some people just like to know that it is out of there, so even if you don't get more performacne it is still worth the piece of mind.||||reg||1
Z||000012||pHoOl||07-29-2001||09:49 PM||||alright, now just cause i'm curious.  i'm assuming that there is an easy way to get it out.  I read that "10 cheap and easy performance mods" list, and he says just bash it with a screwdriver.  Wouldn't be my choice of tools<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Rogue_Ant||07-29-2001||10:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pHoOl:<BR><B>alright, now just cause i'm curious.  i'm assuming that there is an easy way to get it out.  I read that "10 cheap and easy performance mods" list, and he says just bash it with a screwdriver.  Wouldn't be my choice of tools<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lol, that is what I thought at first.  But it is really the best way to do it.  Just stick the screwdriver in and tap it with a hammer, the plastic will break (quite nicely too) and just pull the pieces out.<P>Rogue||||reg||7
Z||000014||Formula88||07-29-2001||11:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pHoOl:<BR><B>alright, now just cause i'm curious.  i'm assuming that there is an easy way to get it out.  I read that "10 cheap and easy performance mods" list, and he says just bash it with a screwdriver.  Wouldn't be my choice of tools<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, well, that article also says the only air the engine gets is going through a hole the size of a flat blad screwdriver bit.  Um, no.  That's a weep hole for the water to drain through.  The airflow goes up and over the wall with the weep hole in it.<P>I took mine out and replaced it with a "Mighty Flow" intake tube that goes from the base of the factory air filter canister to the fender vent.  I can't tell a performance difference, but it sounds better.  But it is louder, and the constant sucking sound may annoy some people.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000015||DecadenceR||07-30-2001||12:26 AM||||it is just so easy to take out the water box. YOu just take off the panel, then it's 2 bolts holding it in. Why break it when you can remove it altogether?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Carrolles||07-31-2001||04:04 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jake_Dragon||07-31-2001||04:52 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Carrolles:<BR><B> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>SNIP 8&lt;----------------------------------<BR>In searching for more power, my race crew tested the Fiero V6 engine on the <BR>dyno without the stock air cleaner and inlet system, and we were surprised <BR>to find that this produced no change in power. Apparently, the Pontiac <BR>engineers did a good job on these parts because they work well, even though <BR>they look restrictive.<BR>END SNIP 8&lt;--------------------------------<P><p>[This message has been edited by Jake_Dragon (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||07-31-2001||05:09 PM||||Well, the 2.8 doesn't draw enough air to notice a restriction.<BR>The 3400TDC and 3800SC, and V8's of course, will benefit from freeing up the intake tract.||||reg||1
